20_no_out_of_bounds_access` | [x] |\n\n### Notes on rows 15–19 (undefined behaviour rows)\n\n* Rows 15–18 are compared by **fatal signal**, in a forked child process\n  (`std::process::Command` re-invoking the test binary), so a mismatch such as\n  \"C segfaults, Rust aborts with a Rust panic message\" is caught rather than\n  hidden. They are asserted against the **release** Rust `.so`, which is the\n  shipping artifact (`[profile.release] panic = \"abort\"`). Rust's *debug*\n  profile intentionally inserts `null_pointer_dereference` /\n  `misaligned_pointer_dereference` UB assertions that abort with a diagnostic\n  instead of faulting; that is a deliberate debug-only Rust language feature,\n  not a translation divergence, so the probes are run against `release` and are\n  skipped (reported) for `debug`.\n* Row 19 likewise only makes sense against `release` for the same reason.\n* Every other row (1–14, 20) is asserted bit-for-bit against **both** the debug\n  and the release Rust `.so`.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
